.TH HEE-SCROB 1 "2026-08-22" "HEE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hee-scrob \- terse now-playing scrobble, real Plex source
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hee scrob
[\fB\-server\fR \fIURL\fR] [\fB\-current\-only\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Queries a real Plex server's
.B /status/sessions
endpoint and prints one line describing what's currently playing. For
a TV episode, prints the real show name, season/episode, episode
title, and year \-\- not just the episode's own title (Plex's
.B title
attribute on an episode element is the episode title, not the show's;
show name lives in
.BR grandparentTitle ).
.PP
For video content, also pulls the real subtitle line active at the
exact playback position from the
.B .srt
sitting next to the media file. No invented dialogue \-\- if there's
no real .srt, there's no subtitle line in the output, and a moment
that genuinely falls in a silent gap prints nothing, correctly.
.PP
Device names: real hostname of the playing device via reverse DNS on
the player's connecting IP, falling back to Plex's own self\-reported
title if that fails. A local override file,
.BR ~/.config/hee/device-names.txt ,
takes priority over both when present.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-server " URL"
Plex server base URL (default \fBhttps://plex.crooked.tcos.us\fR).
.TP
.B \-current\-only
Skip the \(+-5s subtitle context, print only the exact\-moment line.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Never hardcoded, never printed. The token is resolved in this order and
the first hit wins, so the plain
.B hee scrob
needs no wrapper:
.TP
.B 1.
.BR PLEX_TOKEN ,
which is what
.B hee cred \-run plex\-token \-exec
injects, and which is unambiguous because it is named for this
credential.
.B HEE_CRED_PASS
is
.I not
read as the token:
.BR hee\-cred (1)
injects every sealed credential under that one fixed name and this tool
needs two of them, so the documented
.B \-discord
invocation set it to the webhook URL and the old code sent that URL to
the Plex server in an
.B X\-Plex\-Token
header.
.B \-discord
still reads it, where it is unambiguous by construction.
.TP
.B 2.
the sealed
.B plex\-token
credential, opened by this tool itself via
.BR hee\-cred (1).
The store is
.BR HEE_CRED_DIR ,
defaulting to the HOME store
.BR ~/.hee/secrets .
.TP
.B 3.
.BR ~/.config/hee/plex\-token ,
a real file for contexts where gpg cannot run at all. irssi's EXEC
inherits only the env vars present when irssi launched and does not
source a profile, so this needs no agent, no tty and no restart.
.PP
Step 2 exists because requiring a wrapper put two things on the caller
that are easy to get silently wrong: WHICH store \(em
.BR hee\-cred (1)'s
default is relative to the current directory, so a wrapper without an
explicit one works from
.B ~
and dies one
.B cd
later \(em and THAT A WRAPPER EXISTS AT ALL, since a bash alias is
invisible to cron, to
.BR "ssh host cmd" ,
to CI, and to irssi's EXEC, which is this tool's whole point.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HEE_SCROB_PATH_MAP
Where Plex's media paths live on this host. Plex reports a file as its own
container sees it (for example
.BR /data/... ),
and that path means nothing on a host without the same mount. Set
comma\-separated
.I container=local
pairs, per oper, in heerc:
.RS
.nf
export HEE_SCROB_PATH_MAP="/data=/srv/media"
.fi
.RE
.IP
The longest container prefix wins, and a prefix matches only on a path
component boundary, so
.B /data
never matches
.BR /database .
A malformed pair is skipped. When a path neither exists locally nor maps to
something that does,
.B hee scrob
says so on stderr, naming the path and the mappings it tried; stdout stays
the scrobble line. A path that resolves but has no sidecar
.B .srt
stays quiet. human\-execution\-engine#670.
.SH EXAMPLES
The plain form. No wrapper, no environment: the sealed
.B plex\-token
is opened by this tool.
.RS
.nf

hee scrob
np: Family Guy s20e01 \- LASIK Instinct (2021) @ 00:10:40 (Dad TV)

.fi
.RE
.PP
Post the same line to Discord instead of stdout. The webhook is opened
the same way, from the sealed
.B discord\-webhook\-scrob
account.
.RS
.nf

hee scrob \-discord

.fi
.RE
.PP
An explicit wrapper still wins, which is the way to pass a different
token for one run.
.B plexrun
is the interactive convenience defined in
.B ~/.bash_aliases
(dotfiles); it pins the HOME store, because
.BR hee\-cred (1)'s
default store is relative to the current directory:
.RS
.nf

plexrun() { HEE_CRED_DIR="$HOME/.hee/secrets" hee cred \-run plex\-token \-exec "$@"; }

plexrun hee scrob
np: Family Guy s17e09 \- Pawtucket Pete (2018) @ 00:05:48 (Chrome)

.fi
.RE
.PP
That calls
.B hee
directly, on purpose. The version shipped in
.B ~/.bash_aliases
calls the
.B hee_cred_run
shell function instead, which is sourced by
.B tooling/heerc
and therefore exists only in a shell that sources it \(em so pasting that
form into a shell whose
.B ~/.bashrc
does not load heerc gives
.BR "hee_cred_run: command not found" .
The form above needs nothing but
.B hee
on
.BR PATH .
.PP
It takes a command \(em bare
.B plexrun
has nothing to run and prints its usage. And being a shell function it
exists only in an interactive bash, so it is
.I not
available to cron, to
.BR "ssh host cmd" ,
to CI, or to irssi's EXEC. That is why the plain form above exists and
why it is the one to reach for.
.PP
Seal the two credentials once, on a host whose key can open them.
.B \-recipients
is required \(em
.BR hee\-cred (1)
exits with
.B "\-recipients required"
without it \(em and
.B \-seal
reads the secret from an interactive terminal, never a pipe. To seal a new
credential to the same people who can already open an existing one, read
the recipient list off that file rather than retyping key ids:
.RS
.nf

R=$(gpg \-\-list\-packets \-\-list\-only ~/.hee/secrets/plex\-token.gpg \\
    | sed \-n 's/.*keyid \\([0\-9A\-F]*\\).*/\\1/p' | paste \-sd,)

hee cred \-seal discord\-webhook\-scrob \-recipients "$R" \\
  \-dir ~/.hee/secrets

.fi
.RE
.PP
Those ids are encryption
.I subkey
ids, which is what a sealed file records and what
.B \-recipients
accepts. They will not match the primary key ids that
.B "gpg \-\-list\-keys"
shows, and that is expected rather than a mismatch.
.SH IRSSI INTEGRATION
Designed to sit behind an alias:
.PP
.RS
SCROB = "EXEC \-o ~/.local/bin/hee scrob"
.RE
.PP
then just \fB/SCROB\fR in a channel.
.PP
EXEC is the reason the token file exists. irssi inherits only the
environment variables present when irssi itself launched and sources no
profile, so a
.B PLEX_TOKEN
exported afterwards never reaches
.B /SCROB
without a full client restart \(em and a shell function such as
.B plexrun
is not visible to it at all.
.PP
Step 2 of the resolution order usually covers this: the sealed credential
is opened by this tool, needing no wrapper. It needs
.B gpg\-agent
to already hold the key, though, because EXEC has no terminal to prompt
on. The unseal is bounded by a timeout so a cold agent falls through to
the token file rather than hanging the client. For a setup that works
from a cold start, write the file as well:
.RS
.nf

umask 077
mkdir \-p ~/.config/hee
HEE_CRED_DIR="$HOME/.hee/secrets" hee cred \-pass plex\-token \\
  \-exec sh \-c 'printf %s "$HEE_CRED_PASS" > "$HOME/.config/hee/plex\-token"'

.fi
.RE
.PP
That never prints the token and never places it in shell history. It does
put it in plaintext on disk at mode 0600, which is a real downgrade from
sealed and a deliberate trade for a context where gpg cannot run.
